          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_0.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.084,
            0.113,
            0.101
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.057,
            0.106,
            0.198,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "The charges on the ions are not indicated in the formula of the compound."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.228,
            0.087,
            0.842,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Write the charges under the symbols rather than as superscripts."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.107,
            0.888,
            0.143
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Crossover the charges (only the numbers) as shown below to obtain the formula."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.145,
            0.89,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "(iv) The chemical formula gives the simplest ratio of the elements in a compound. Therefore, after criss-crossing, the subscripts are divided by a common factor, if any. For example, if we get the subscripts 2 and 4, they are divided by 2 to get 1 and 2, which are then used as subscripts in the formula."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.244,
            0.329,
            0.263
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Examples"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.212,
            0.268,
            0.527,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": "The formula of calcium chloride"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.289,
            0.287,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "Symbol"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.324,
            0.283,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": "Charge"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.289,
            0.459,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.356,
            0.295,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.357,
            0.422,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": "CaCl<sub>2</sub>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.211,
            0.378,
            0.857,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "Thus, in calcium chloride, there are two chloride ions (Cl–) for each calcium ion $( \\mathbb { C } \\mathbf { a } ^ { 2 + } )$ The positive and negative charges must balance each <u>other, and the overall structure must be neutral.</u>"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.441,
            0.51,
            0.46
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "The formula of aluminium oxide —"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.441,
            0.865,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "The formula for magnesium oxide"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.462,
            0.287,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "Symbol"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.495,
            0.284,
            0.515
          ],
          "content": "Charge"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.519,
            0.295,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.539,
            0.465,
            0.613,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": "Symbol"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.541,
            0.5,
            0.61,
            0.52
          ],
          "content": "Charge"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.625,
            0.465,
            0.766,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.538,
            0.525,
            0.858,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "Here, the valencies of the two elements are the same. We arrive at the formula $\\mathrm { M g _ { 2 } O _ { 2 } }$ but it is simply written as MgO."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.618,
            0.888,
            0.654
          ],
          "content": "This method can also be used to write formulae of compounds of metals with other polyatomic ions, such as calcium carbonate."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.656,
            0.888,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "For magnesium hydroxide, we write the symbol of the cation $( \\mathrm { M g ^ { 2 + } } )$ first, followed by the symbol of the anion (OH–). Then, their charges (only the numbers) are criss-crossed to get the formula."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.203,
            0.72,
            0.312,
            0.74
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Examples"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.745,
            0.538,
            0.765
          ],
          "content": "The formula for calcium carbonate"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.742,
            0.843,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "The formula of magnesium"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.767,
            0.272,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "Symbol"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.795,
            0.268,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": "Charge"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.765,
            0.467,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_30.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.76,
            0.716,
            0.778
          ],
          "content": "hydroxide"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.78,
            0.67,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "Symbol"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.822,
            0.281,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.362,
            0.822,
            0.42,
            0.842
          ],
          "content": "CaCO<sub>3</sub>"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.691,
            0.782,
            0.838,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_image_35.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.816,
            0.667,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": "Charge"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.843,
            0.576,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "Here, the valencies of the two ions are the same. The formula $\\mathrm{Ca}_{2}(\\mathrm{CO}_{3})_{2}$ is simply written as $\\mathsf { C a C O } _ { 3 ^ { \\prime } }$ as explained above."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.595,
            0.844,
            0.676,
            0.863
          ],
          "content": "Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.733,
            0.845,
            0.814,
